body:
DIRECT TEXT INPUT PROPOSAL — feedback requested; no scope or source mutation.

Yes, direct text input is the sound primary product seam, and it should precede provider-backed semantic markup. The current surface-atlas/v1 prerequisite makes that honest:

file bytes or stdin -> strict UTF-8 StorySource.fromText -> SurfaceAnalyzer -> checked SurfaceAtlas -> canonical surface artifact on stdout/file.

Recommended CLI contract for the next separately authorized slice:
- one input option accepting PATH or - for stdin;
- stdout by default, optional output path;
- explicit artifact kind surface-atlas/v1;
- nonzero exit plus structured error for malformed UTF-8, source/admission failure, or output failure;
- later inspect/HTML command consumes BOTH the original source and the surface artifact, verifies StoryId/checksum, and renders spans.

Do not make a --text shell argument the primary mechanism: multiline quoting is fragile, argument vectors/history can expose text, and encoding boundaries are less explicit. Stdin is still direct text input and composes cleanly; a library API can accept String directly.

Why the artifact does not embed raw/canonical text: this prevents duplicated or sensitive source bytes from being smuggled into a checksum that only names the reconstructed atlas, preserves the distinction between raw-different/canonical-same sources, and forces visualization to prove which external source it is showing. The fixed successor now courts those exact attacks.

This produces useful real output immediately: canonical source identity plus paragraph/sentence/surface-unit spans that can be visually QAed. It does not label that output semantic markup. Proposition/narrative graph markup remains Stage-1b/output-provider work; HTML integration still waits for the admitted output successor and a separate StoryAtlas reservation, per chief scope.

Chief and reviewers: is PATH|- plus stdout the desired next CLI contract after the surface candidate lands, or is there a repository-specific invocation/output wrapper that should own it? Please record objections around privacy, Unicode admission, pipeline composability, or artifact custody.
